#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162917} :activity/id #uuid "6dfe6766-3597-55f1-8e7d-7819e9f40a12" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048404 :topic/id #uuid "9c5255d1-f9cb-56c9-a2a3-c2e55a417137" :topic/math-academy-id 3010} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162917 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163251 :tutorial/content "A discrete random variable $X$ follows a **discrete uniform distribution** over a set $S$ if each value in $S$ is equally likely. \n\nThe probability mass function for the discrete uniform distribution on a set $S$ is given by\n\n![](images/61/61fd8cf495b429392260fbad1b671c9c48c8f681827653aaabe73a4dbf6b6ecf.svg)\n\nWhen a random variable $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over the set $S=\\{ s_1, s_2, \\ldots, s_n\\},$ we denote it as\n\n$$\nX \\sim U \\left\\{ s_1, s_2, \\ldots, s_n\\right\\}\n$$\n\nand we say that $X$ is **uniformly distributed** over $S.$\n\nFor example, consider the random variable $T\\sim U\\{1,2,4\\}.$ In this case, $S = \\{1,2,4\\},$ $|S| = 3,$ and therefore the probability mass function for $T$ is\n\n![](images/d1/d19075d50e24a2fc9d157311535d0665103b1498ef99312606c4cf6c482cfe1a.svg)\n\nA sketch of $f(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/cb/cbac12f164635cd4efaa94167a4582689d470c3373769e1aa9facc2670f732ef.png)\n\n**Note:** In this lesson, we will make use of the following formula for computing the cardinality of a set of consecutive numbers:\n\n$$\n\\left| \\{ a, a+1, a+2, \\ldots, b \\} \\right| = b - a + 1\n$$\n\nDon't forget about the $+1$ at the end of the formula. The $+1$ is needed because we are including both endpoints $a$ and $b$ in the count. (Not convinced? Notice that $\\left| \\{ 4, 5 \\} \\right| = 5 - 4 + 1 = 2.$)" :tutorial/id #uuid "09eacd15-bdd6-56f7-a6b3-5f9659e21543" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5029 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c15691c5-424a-5fd1-807b-8d1b58e97b62" :step/math-academy-id 31449 :step/next {:db/id #e